# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# existence-proves-aliens (Miya's Note). Script from miya 2026-11-08; fact-checked per guideline 12-1 (see script-check.md).
SCENES = [
 ('S01', 'Look Down', 'ill', 'p01a', 'Look down at your hands for a moment. Feel the warmth of your skin and the steady rhythm of your breathing. Right now, you are sitting on a spinning ball of rock, hurtling through a cold and silent void.'),
 ('S02', 'Look Down', 'ill', 'p01b', 'It feels completely normal to you because it is the only reality you have ever known. But have you ever paused to ask why you are here to experience it at all?'),
 ('S03', 'Look Down', 'ill', 'p01c', 'What if the simple fact that you are alive right now is a powerful clue that we are not alone?'),
 ('S04', 'The Quiet Sky', 'ill', 'p02a', 'Most people look up at the night sky and search for distant radio signals or mysterious lights. We build colossal telescopes, scan distant star systems, and wonder why the universe seems so quiet. It looks like a frustrating paradox.'),
 ('S05', 'The Quiet Sky', 'ill', 'p02b', 'If the cosmos is so vast, why have we never found a single confirmed trace of alien life? But what if we have been looking at the puzzle completely backward?'),
 ('S06', 'The Quiet Sky', 'ill', 'p02c', 'What if the most convincing piece of evidence for extraterrestrial life is not out there in deep space, but sitting right in your chair?'),
 ('S07', 'Made of Stars', 'ill', 'p03a', 'Think about what your existence actually means. You are not an outside observer looking into the universe; you are a piece of it.'),
 ('S08', 'Made of Stars', 'ill', 'p03b', 'The hydrogen in your body dates back to the Big Bang, and nearly every heavier atom, from the calcium in your bones to the iron in your blood, was forged inside stars or scattered by their explosions.'),
 ('S09', 'Made of Stars', 'ill', 'p03c', 'You are made of the same raw material that fills the rest of the cosmos. If ordinary cosmic matter woke up and became conscious here on Earth, why would anyone assume it could only happen once?'),
 ('S10', 'The Scale of It All', 'ill', 'p04a', 'Consider the sheer scale of the playground we inhabit. Our home galaxy, the Milky Way, contains somewhere between one hundred billion and four hundred billion stars. Let that number sink in. And our galaxy is not unique.'),
 ('S11', 'The Scale of It All', 'ill', 'p04b', 'Estimates for the observable universe run from about two hundred billion to two trillion galaxies, each with its own crowd of suns. How could an entire ocean of matter produce only one single drop of life?'),
 ('S12', 'Planets Everywhere', 'ill', 'p05a', 'For generations, skeptics argued that planets might be extraordinarily rare, and that our solar system was a freak accident, never to be repeated. But modern science has shaken that belief.'),
 ('S13', 'Planets Everywhere', 'ill', 'p05b', 'Space telescopes have discovered thousands of confirmed exoplanets orbiting distant stars, and surveys suggest that, on average, a star hosts at least one planet. Rocky planets in the habitable zone, where liquid water could exist, appear to be common, not miraculous.'),
 ('S14', 'Planets Everywhere', 'ill', 'p05c', "NASA's Kepler space telescope alone found thousands of planets, and some of them are roughly Earth-sized and orbit in their star's habitable zone."),
 ('S15', 'Life Came Early', 'ill', 'p06a', 'Even more striking is the timeline of our own planet. Earth formed roughly four and a half billion years ago, amid violent collisions of molten rock and toxic air.'),
 ('S16', 'Life Came Early', 'ill', 'p06b', 'Yet the oldest widely accepted evidence of life is about three and a half billion years old, and some researchers argue for traces that are older still. That suggests life arose fairly early, once conditions allowed.'),
 ('S17', 'Life Came Early', 'ill', 'p06c', 'It does not prove that life is common, since we only have one example. But it is a hint that biology may not be a long shot.'),
 ('S18', 'Nothing Special', 'ill', 'p07a', 'This brings us to one of the most basic principles in science, the Copernican Principle. Simply put, it says that Earth does not occupy a special, privileged position in the universe.'),
 ('S19', 'Nothing Special', 'ill', 'p07b', 'The laws of physics that govern the orbit of the Moon appear to operate in the farthest galaxies too. Carbon behaves like carbon everywhere, and water is found across the cosmos.'),
 ('S20', 'Nothing Special', 'ill', 'p07c', 'If the rules of the game are identical everywhere, why would the outcome be unique to us?'),
 ('S21', 'The Handful of Sand', 'ill', 'p08a', 'Imagine walking along an endless sandy shoreline that stretches for thousands of miles. You bend down, scoop up a single handful of wet sand, and discover a tiny living sea creature wriggling inside your palm.'),
 ('S22', 'The Handful of Sand', 'ill', 'p08b', "Would you stand up, look out at the thousands of miles of identical coast, and conclude that your hand holds the only living organism on the entire planet? Of course you wouldn't."),
 ('S23', 'The Handful of Sand', 'ill', 'p08c', 'That one creature is a strong hint that the shoreline naturally supports life.'),
 ('S24', 'Zero or One', 'ill', 'p09a', 'Earth is that single handful of sand. We are a sample size of one, but a sample size of one is infinitely greater than a sample size of zero.'),
 ('S25', 'Zero or One', 'ill', 'p09b', 'In probability, zero means an event is impossible, and once an event has happened even one time, it is clearly possible. Of course, one example cannot tell us exactly how common life is.'),
 ('S26', 'Zero or One', 'ill', 'p09c', 'But a natural process that has worked once, in a universe governed by the same laws everywhere, is very likely to happen again.'),
 ('S27', 'The Ingredients', 'ill', 'p10a', 'Think about the ingredients life needs. We rely on carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, phosphorus, and sulfur. Hydrogen, oxygen, carbon, and nitrogen are among the most abundant elements in the entire universe, and phosphorus and sulfur are widespread too.'),
 ('S28', 'The Ingredients', 'ill', 'p10b', 'These are not rare, exotic materials hidden in obscure corners of space. The raw recipe for living cells turns up in nebulae, asteroids, and interstellar clouds. The cosmic kitchen is well stocked.'),
 ('S29', 'Building Blocks in Space', 'ill', 'p11a', 'We also find organic molecules floating freely in deep space. Radio telescopes have detected complex carbon compounds, including precursors of amino acids, inside giant clouds of gas.'),
 ('S30', 'Building Blocks in Space', 'ill', 'p11b', 'Amino acids, the building blocks of proteins, have been found in meteorites, in samples from the comet Wild 2, and in material brought back from the asteroids Ryugu and Bennu.'),
 ('S31', 'Building Blocks in Space', 'ill', 'p11c', 'Scientists think many of these molecules formed out in space, in the freezing dark, with no planet needed at all.'),
 ('S32', 'Life Without Sunlight', 'ill', 'p12a', "Now consider how tough life is right here on Earth. For a long time, scientists assumed that living things could only survive in gentle places like sunlit meadows and calm seas. But explorers of Earth's harshest corners were stunned to discover extremophiles."),
 ('S33', 'Life Without Sunlight', 'ill', 'p12b', 'These are microbes that live in scalding hydrothermal vents on the ocean floor, deep inside solid rock, in radioactive waste, and in lakes buried beneath Antarctic ice.'),
 ('S34', 'Life Without Sunlight', 'ill', 'p12c', 'One microbe has even been grown in the lab at one hundred and twenty-two degrees Celsius, hotter than boiling water.'),
 ('S35', 'Hidden Oceans', 'ill', 'p13a', 'Many of these microbes need no sunlight at all. They feed on chemicals like sulfur and hydrogen, powered by the heat of the Earth itself. Their existence rewrote our understanding of biological limits.'),
 ('S36', 'Hidden Oceans', 'ill', 'p13b', 'If microbes can flourish around vents on the bottom of our own oceans, the frozen moons of our solar system look much less barren.'),
 ('S37', 'Hidden Oceans', 'ill', 'p13c', "Evidence strongly suggests that Jupiter's moon Europa and Saturn's moon Enceladus hide oceans of liquid water beneath their icy crusts, kept from freezing in part by tidal friction. Some scientists think life on Earth may have begun near similar vents."),
 ('S38', 'Hidden Oceans', 'ill', 'p13d', 'No one has found life on those moons yet, but they are exciting places to look.'),
 ('S39', 'Hidden Oceans', 'ill', 'p13e', "In 2005, the Cassini spacecraft spotted jets of water vapor and ice erupting from Enceladus, and later flybys through those plumes found salts, organic molecules, and hydrogen. NASA's Europa Clipper, launched in 2024, is now on its way to study Europa."),
 ('S40', 'The Fermi Paradox', 'ill', 'p14a', 'So, if the ingredients are common and the physical laws are identical, why does the night sky seem so quiet?'),
 ('S41', 'The Fermi Paradox', 'ill', 'p14b', 'This mystery is famously known as the Fermi Paradox, after the physicist Enrico Fermi, who is said to have asked over lunch in 1950, where is everybody? If extraterrestrial civilizations are likely, why do we see no sign of them?'),
 ('S42', 'The Fermi Paradox', 'ill', 'p14c', 'Many people read this eerie silence as proof that we must be alone. But there is a simpler, more humbling explanation that many scientists take seriously. The universe is not empty.'),
 ('S43', 'The Fermi Paradox', 'ill', 'p14d', 'It is simply incomprehensibly large, and our time in it is a momentary whisper.'),
 ('S44', 'Cosmic Distance', 'ill', 'p15a', 'Consider the reality of cosmic distance. Light travels at roughly three hundred thousand kilometers per second, the ultimate speed limit of the universe. Yet even at that blinding speed, it takes light about one hundred thousand years to cross our galaxy.'),
 ('S45', 'Cosmic Distance', 'ill', 'p15b', 'By comparison, the nearest star beyond the sun is more than four light-years away, and Voyager 1, the most distant spacecraft we have ever launched, would need roughly seventy thousand years to get there.'),
 ('S46', 'Cosmic Distance', 'ill', 'p15c', 'Human beings have only been broadcasting radio signals into space for roughly one century. That means our technological signature is a bubble just a couple of hundred light-years across, a nearly invisible speck against the grand expanse of the Milky Way.'),
 ('S47', 'What Aliens Would See', 'ill', 'p16a', 'Suppose an advanced civilization on the opposite side of our galaxy aimed a giant telescope at us right now. They would not pick up our radio broadcasts, our internet signals, or our television shows.'),
 ('S48', 'What Aliens Would See', 'ill', 'p16b', 'The light reaching them would have left Earth about one hundred thousand years ago, during the last ice age, when early modern humans and Neanderthals were still hunting with stone tools.'),
 ('S49', 'What Aliens Would See', 'ill', 'p16c', 'To them, Earth would look like a world without technology, completely unaware of the digital society we have built.'),
 ('S50', 'The Time Barrier', 'ill', 'p17a', 'Time is an even greater barrier than distance. The universe is about thirteen point eight billion years old.'),
 ('S51', 'The Time Barrier', 'ill', 'p17b', 'If that entire history were squeezed into a single calendar year, all of recorded human history would fill only the last ten seconds or so before midnight on December thirty-first.'),
 ('S52', 'The Time Barrier', 'ill', 'p17c', 'How likely is it that two civilizations would flourish at the exact same cosmic moment, close enough to shake hands across the stars?'),
 ('S53', 'The Silent Forest', 'ill', 'p18a', 'Other civilizations may have risen, built incredible wonders, and vanished long before our sun even formed. Others might not begin their journey until long after our sun has burned out. The silence does not mean the cosmic forest is empty.'),
 ('S54', 'The Silent Forest', 'ill', 'p18b', 'When you walk through a dark, quiet forest at midnight, you do not assume you are the only living creature on Earth.'),
 ('S55', 'The Silent Forest', 'ill', 'p18c', 'You recognize that the forest is vast, the shadows are deep, and other creatures are simply minding their own lives out of sight.'),
 ('S56', 'Too Early?', 'ill', 'p19a', 'Some thinkers propose an even deeper idea: that humanity might be among the earliest intelligent species to emerge. The first stars after the Big Bang were made almost entirely of hydrogen and helium.'),
 ('S57', 'Too Early?', 'ill', 'p19b', 'Over billions of years, generation after generation of stars lived, died, and exploded, slowly enriching the cosmos with carbon, oxygen, and iron. Rocky planets and complex life needed that chemical foundation. This is a hypothesis, not a fact.'),
 ('S58', 'Too Early?', 'ill', 'p19c', 'But if it is right, we might not be late to a crowded cosmic party. We might be walking into the room just as the music begins to play.'),
 ('S59', 'The Opening Note', 'ill', 'p20a', 'Perhaps the universe is only now entering the era when intelligent life can awaken and look out at the stars. Stars could keep forming for trillions of years. If so, our existence is not a lonely miracle.'),
 ('S60', 'The Opening Note', 'ill', 'p20b', 'It could be the opening note of a vast cosmic symphony that has barely begun.'),
 ('S61', 'Stardust All Around', 'ill', 'p21a', 'Every breath you take connects you to the cosmos. When you wake up tomorrow morning, pour your coffee, and step outside into the crisp air, look around you.'),
 ('S62', 'Stardust All Around', 'ill', 'p21b', 'Notice the trees, the birds chirping on the branches, the dirt beneath your shoes, and the commuters hurrying down the street.'),
 ('S63', 'Stardust All Around', 'ill', 'p21c', 'Every one of these living things is built from recycled stardust, shaped by the chemistry and physics that operate in every corner of space.'),
 ('S64', 'The Clue Is You', 'ill', 'p22a', 'You are not an isolated accident. You are evidence of what the universe does when given enough time, energy, and matter. We are the cosmos waking up, growing eyes to see its own beauty, and a mind to ponder its own origins.'),
 ('S65', 'The Clue Is You', 'ill', 'p22b', 'If the rules of nature allowed matter to think, feel, and dream here, those same rules make it very likely that someone else, on a world around a distant star, is doing the same thing right now.'),
 ('S66', 'The Clue Is You', 'ill', 'p22c', 'The clue was never hidden in a mysterious radio signal from deep space. The clue is you.'),
]
